foreign  [Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Tuesday November 15th I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  after years of arguing against  regulation a lot of people in the crypto  industry lately have been pushing for  oversight with a lighter touch but the  crisis at FTX has put plans for some new  crypto legislation on hold  according to wsj sources the Senate  agriculture committee which oversees the  federal agency that could be tasked with  regulating crypto has scrapped a  tentative plan to mark up its bipartisan  crypto bill this week a bill that had  been drafted with ftx's input at the  same time Regulators are expected to  increase their oversight and  investigations of crypto firms hoping to  give investors in the space a little  more security joining us to discuss what  this means for the sector is our  reporter in Washington Paul Kiernan hi  Paul hi Zoe Paul can you start by  telling us a bit about what was the  point of the crypto legislation that the  Senate agriculture committee was looking  at and what was in it and why were they  considering it  so at a high level the Senate  agriculture committee bill it's called  the digital Commodities consumer  protection act or the dccpa what it  would have done is it would have given  the commodity Futures Trading commission  jurisdiction over Bitcoin and ether and  probably a few other cryptocurrencies  the cftc as that regulator is known is  the smaller of the two Market Regulators  the bigger one is the Securities and  Exchange Commission and it does not have  any rules in place currently that it  could really  use to regulate these markets and so one  thing we know is that if this bill were  passed it would mean several years of  rule writing efforts by the commodity  Futures Trading commission to like build  this regulatory framework for crypto and  of course during that time the crypto  industry would be heavily lobbying the  cftc and Congress and what would the  bill have meant for other crypto tokens  besides Bitcoin and ether there were  various versions and drafts of the spill  circulating but it would have preserved  the ability for the Securities and  Exchange Commission to say wait no that  looks like a security that's in our  jurisdiction and go after crypto  exchanges for listing crypto tokens that  met the definition of a security Now one  reason the SEC didn't like the CCPA  is that it would have deferred to the  crypto exchanges in a first instance for  determining what type of asset a given  crypto token was so this would have  given crypto exchanges like FTX and  others the power to decide whether or  not something was a security or a  commodity something that usually  Regulators get to do  yeah right I mean theoretically it  wouldn't have given the crypto exchanges  the final say the SEC could still come  in and second guess their decision but  it would have deferred to a certain  degree  this distinction to the crypto industry  again there were various versions of it  that were circulating and tweaks being  made and so you know a final version of  the bill is not in existence because  they haven't voted on it there is an  expectation that Regulators like the SEC  will step up their oversight of the  industry particularly given what's  happened at FTX how do we expect that to  play out for the past year the crypto  industry in Washington has gotten a lot  more organized with lobbyists and  spending and the result of that has been  more and more lawmakers Republicans like  Tom emmer Democrats like Richie Torres  many more repeating crypto lobbyist  talking points one of which is the SEC  should not do regulation by enforcement  rather than enforcing the existing laws  the SEC should provide more regulatory  Clarity to the crypto industry to tell  crypto companies exactly what they can  do not just what they can't do the SEC  has maintained for several years now  that the laws are clear that there is a  really pretty straightforward test as  called the Howie test that determines  when something is a security and when  it's not and the crypto industry is just  trying to kick the can down the road one  upshot of the FTX implosion is that it's  going to push the needle towards  enforcement let's take a moment to talk  about the investigations into FDX who is  looking into them right now and what are  they looking to find  yeah so the SEC is investigating FTX  they were frankly investigating FTX  before this happened but the  investigation has lightened in the wake  of the collapse they are looking into  whether FTX listed unregistered  Securities whether it's lending product  constituted an unregistered security  more recently they've been looking at  the interconnectedness the links between  ftx.com which is the global Exchange  ftx.us which is its U.S subsidiary and  Sam bankman Freed's trading firm  alimator research we've reported that  FTX The Exchange lent customer funds to  elevator research which is generally  forbidden what other crypto related  investigations have we seen the SEC and  you know other agencies carrying out and  what do we expect the impact of those to  be it's hard to say at this point we've  reported that the SEC was investigating  Finance coinbase which is a publicly  traded company as well as a crypto  exchange has disclosed that it was under  SEC investigation for like various lines  of business SEC chair Gary Gensler wants  the crypto exchanges to register as  National Security exchanges  there's a number of reasons why they  don't want to do that and why that would  be difficult but he has expressed a  willingness to work with these companies  to try to get them registered there's  some risk if the SEC were to file a  lawsuit against the crypto exchange  particularly one whose finances aren't  audited and publicly disclosed there's  some risk that it could precipitate  a collapse or that it would like a cease  and desist order might cause the  exchanges customers to lose access to  their money and so  I think that from the sec's perspective  the best thing would just be to register  all these companies and get them to  comply with the law but Gary ensler said  if they don't do that if they don't make  any Headway and there's nothing public  that has come out that suggests there's  a lot of Headway being made the SEC will  bring Enforcement cases it'll sue them  all right that's our reporter Paul  Kiernan thanks for joining us Paul  thanks for having me  and that's it for today's Tech news  briefing if you want more Tech stories  head over to our website wsj.com I'm Zoe  Thomas for The Wall Street Journal  thanks so much for listening  foreign  [Music]

